 "verdict": "CENSORED, and the sweep says WHY. The median signal-to-noise ratio is 0.53-1.40 across three decades of delta -- the mean difference never clears one standard error at any separation. Crucially the apparent growth of |mean|/delta (0.062 at the largest delta to 0.936 at the smallest) is NOT evidence of divergence: |mean| sits at the noise floor throughout, and CRN makes that floor shrink with delta, so the ratio inherits a 1/delta trend for a purely statistical reason. Since the SNR is FLAT rather than improving as delta shrinks, refining the separation cannot rescue this route; only K can, and SNR grows as sqrt(K), so resolving at SNR 3 needs about 5x the replicates used here."
